WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.217
Think of a night when someone you
love was unwell, and you weren't sure whether

2
00:00:05.217 --> 00:00:09.605
what you were seeing was normal,
or whether you should call someone.

3
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.395
Now picture that night
in a small town in Tamil Nadu.

4
00:00:14.395 --> 00:00:17.094
A woman is in pain in the back room.

5
00:00:17.094 --> 00:00:21.219
Her daughter sits beside
her, not sure what to do next.

6
00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:25.963
The local health worker,
an ASHA, has walked over after dark.

7
00:00:25.963 --> 00:00:28.995
She listens, and
the question comes in Tamil.

8
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.586
Her pain is worse
at night. What should we do?

9
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.258
Questions like this
come up in homes across India.

10
00:00:38.258 --> 00:00:41.823
Often, no one who could
answer it is in the room.

11
00:00:43.053 --> 00:00:46.513
Palliative care is the
part of medicine that eases pain,

12
00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:50.405
breathlessness and fear for
people living with serious illness.

13
00:00:50.405 --> 00:00:53.926
It is about living as well
as possible, right to the end.

14
00:00:54.653 --> 00:00:57.269
Much of that care can happen at home,

15
00:00:57.269 --> 00:01:01.934
with family, and with the community
health workers who visit them.

16
00:01:01.934 --> 00:01:07.023
In rural India, the first link
to health care is usually an ASHA worker.

17
00:01:07.753 --> 00:01:09.512
And here is the difficulty.

18
00:01:09.512 --> 00:01:14.593
India has more than a million ASHA
workers, all of them women, and they get no

19
00:01:14.593 --> 00:01:17.003
standard training in palliative care.

20
00:01:17.003 --> 00:01:21.432
The knowledge they need exists, but it
is written mostly in English.

21
00:01:22.153 --> 00:01:27.047
Two things make this the moment to
try. A voice call from an ordinary phone,

22
00:01:27.047 --> 00:01:31.425
even on a weak 2G connection,
can now reach a computer that listens.

23
00:01:31.425 --> 00:01:36.254
And speech technology can now recognise
speech in all twenty two of India's

24
00:01:36.254 --> 00:01:40.246
scheduled languages, and reply aloud
in eleven of them so far.

25
00:01:40.246 --> 00:01:42.757
The others fall back to Hindi, for now.

26
00:01:43.586 --> 00:01:48.302
That opens a possibility. What
if a health worker, standing in that room,

27
00:01:48.302 --> 00:01:53.341
could simply ask her question out
loud, in her own language, and hear careful,

28
00:01:53.341 --> 00:01:55.214
evidence-based guidance back?

29
00:01:55.953 --> 00:02:00.773
A team of doctors and engineers
built a tool that tries to do exactly that.

30
00:02:00.773 --> 00:02:03.601
They are not asking you to
believe it works.

31
00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:06.364
They are asking
for the chance to find out.

32
00:02:06.364 --> 00:02:10.027
That honesty runs through
everything you're about to see.

33
00:02:10.753 --> 00:02:15.258
So how big is the gap between that
question and an answer?

34
00:02:17.787 --> 00:02:21.377
Start with the people who need
this care. In India,

35
00:02:21.377 --> 00:02:23.982
that is more than ten million people.

36
00:02:23.982 --> 00:02:27.573
Each of these dots stands for
ten thousand of them:

37
00:02:27.573 --> 00:02:30.178
a parent, a grandparent, a neighbour.

38
00:02:30.887 --> 00:02:33.571
If ten million people need this care,

39
00:02:33.571 --> 00:02:37.635
roughly how many get it
from someone trained to give it?

40
00:02:40.854 --> 00:02:44.686
Fewer than two in every
hundred. Of these thousand dots,

41
00:02:44.686 --> 00:02:49.817
fewer than twenty would light up. A Lancet
Commission gave this gap a name:

42
00:02:49.817 --> 00:02:50.981
the access abyss.

43
00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.138
It is not only India.

44
00:02:53.138 --> 00:02:57.909
The World Health Organization estimates
that fifty seven million people need

45
00:02:57.909 --> 00:03:03.120
palliative care each year, and seventy eight
percent of them live in low and middle

46
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.188
income countries.

47
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.050
Pain relief shows the gap starkly.

48
00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:12.251
India is home to seventeen percent of
the world's people, but it uses less than one

49
00:03:12.251 --> 00:03:17.076
percent of the world's medical opioids, the
medicines that treat severe pain.

50
00:03:17.887 --> 00:03:23.616
So who could close this gap? There is already a
workforce that reaches the villages:

51
00:03:23.616 --> 00:03:29.141
more than a million ASHA workers, the primary
link to health care in rural India.

52
00:03:29.854 --> 00:03:31.170
The workforce exists.

53
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:35.870
What's missing is the training,
and the knowledge in a language they speak.

54
00:03:37.687 --> 00:03:42.019
India has twenty two constitutionally
scheduled languages. But the guidelines,

55
00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:46.130
handbooks and protocols for palliative
care are written mostly in English.

56
00:03:46.854 --> 00:03:52.482
So when the question from that room
travels toward the knowledge, it hits a wall.

57
00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:58.213
Think about what that means in
practice. A worker may read some English.

58
00:03:58.213 --> 00:04:01.747
But a clinical handbook
in English, read in a hurry,

59
00:04:01.747 --> 00:04:05.689
beside a patient in pain,
is a different thing altogether.

60
00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.322
There is a well-known way
around this wall.

61
00:04:09.322 --> 00:04:14.723
Kerala built a community model in which
trained volunteers bring palliative care

62
00:04:14.723 --> 00:04:16.410
close to almost everyone.

63
00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:17.018
It works.

64
00:04:17.018 --> 00:04:22.351
But it depends on intensive human
training, and that is very hard to scale to a

65
00:04:22.351 --> 00:04:23.296
whole country.

66
00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:24.554
What if,

67
00:04:24.554 --> 00:04:30.100
instead of teaching everyone the whole book,
the expertise could speak her language

68
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.905
at the moment she needs it?

69
00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.334
That is the idea behind Palli Sahayak.

70
00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:41.907
It is a voice-first clinical decision support
tool for palliative care, and it is

71
00:04:41.907 --> 00:04:42.733
open source.

72
00:04:43.453 --> 00:04:48.530
There are three ways in: an ordinary
phone call, WhatsApp, or the web.

73
00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:54.333
No new app to learn, no screen to navigate.
Her hands stay free for the patient.

74
00:04:55.053 --> 00:04:56.916
Notice what it does not do.

75
00:04:56.916 --> 00:05:01.816
It does not examine the patient, and it
does not replace her judgement.

76
00:05:01.816 --> 00:05:07.060
It answers a question, the way a
knowledgeable colleague on the phone might.

77
00:05:07.787 --> 00:05:12.825
Follow the question. Her voice becomes a
waveform. The waveform becomes text.

78
00:05:12.825 --> 00:05:17.601
The text is used to search a curated
library of palliative care guidance.

79
00:05:17.601 --> 00:05:20.545
And what comes back
is a short spoken answer.

80
00:05:21.253 --> 00:05:23.831
The answers are designed to be short:

81
00:05:23.831 --> 00:05:28.291
under thirty seconds of speech, so
they fit inside a home visit.

82
00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.378
The library is not the open internet.

83
00:05:31.378 --> 00:05:36.603
Today it holds a national palliative
care handbook for community health workers, a

84
00:05:36.603 --> 00:05:41.510
guide to caring for patients at home, and a
Pallium India compilation on pain

85
00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:42.147
medicines.

86
00:05:42.887 --> 00:05:48.826
The tool was built under a Gates Foundation
and BIRAC Grand Challenges India grant,

87
00:05:48.826 --> 00:05:52.261
and its code is open
for anyone to read and use.

88
00:05:53.887 --> 00:06:00.371
How does a machine find the right answer? It
helps to think about two kinds of exam.

89
00:06:00.371 --> 00:06:03.844
In a closed-book
exam you answer from memory.

90
00:06:03.844 --> 00:06:07.395
In an open-book exam
you look things up first.

91
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.428
Palli Sahayak is built to take
the open-book exam.

92
00:06:11.428 --> 00:06:15.465
This is called retrieval-augmented
generation: look up first,

93
00:06:15.465 --> 00:06:18.245
then write the answer from what was found.

94
00:06:18.954 --> 00:06:23.512
So how do you look things up
by meaning, rather than by exact words?

95
00:06:23.512 --> 00:06:27.601
Each passage in the library
is turned into a list of numbers,

96
00:06:27.601 --> 00:06:31.758
which you can picture as an
arrow pointing somewhere in space.

97
00:06:32.487 --> 00:06:35.005
Here we draw them in two dimensions.

98
00:06:35.005 --> 00:06:39.622
Really there are hundreds of
dimensions, but the idea is the same.

99
00:06:40.254 --> 00:06:42.481
The question becomes an arrow too.

100
00:06:42.481 --> 00:06:47.524
Passages about similar things point
in similar directions, so we look for the

101
00:06:47.524 --> 00:06:50.602
arrows with the smallest
angle to the question.

102
00:06:50.602 --> 00:06:54.139
That closeness is measured by
the cosine of the angle.

103
00:06:54.954 --> 00:07:00.105
Let's make it concrete. Suppose the question is
about pain that gets worse at night.

104
00:07:00.105 --> 00:07:03.601
One passage, on night-time pain,
points ten degrees away.

105
00:07:03.601 --> 00:07:07.649
Another, on pain medicines in general,
points twenty degrees away.

106
00:07:07.649 --> 00:07:11.084
A third, on breathlessness,
points seventy degrees away.

107
00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:17.002
The cosine of ten degrees is about
zero point nine eight. Of twenty degrees,

108
00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:22.526
about zero point nine four. Of seventy
degrees, only about zero point three four.

109
00:07:22.526 --> 00:07:27.572
So the first two passages are retrieved,
and the third stays on the shelf.

110
00:07:28.387 --> 00:07:32.517
Why compare the angle,
and not the length of the arrows?

111
00:07:35.754 --> 00:07:39.049
Because the direction is what
carries the meaning.

112
00:07:39.049 --> 00:07:44.584
Two passages can be about the same thing and
still have arrows of different lengths.

113
00:07:44.584 --> 00:07:49.263
The angle ignores that, and asks only:
are these pointing the same way?

114
00:07:50.087 --> 00:07:52.640
Then comes the rule that matters most.

115
00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:56.135
The answer has to stand
on those retrieved passages.

116
00:07:56.135 --> 00:08:01.713
If the library has nothing relevant, the right
answer is to say so, and to point to

117
00:08:01.713 --> 00:08:02.520
a clinician.

118
00:08:03.354 --> 00:08:06.933
Only then does a language model
compose a short answer,

119
00:08:06.933 --> 00:08:12.270
from those passages, and attach an evidence
badge that grades confidence from A to

120
00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:12.400
E.

121
00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.223
Retrieval first, then words.

122
00:08:15.254 --> 00:08:18.894
In one configuration being tested,
there's one more check.

123
00:08:18.894 --> 00:08:23.288
A knowledge graph, a map linking
symptoms, medicines and side effects,

124
00:08:23.288 --> 00:08:25.359
double-checks the drafted answer.

125
00:08:25.359 --> 00:08:30.632
If the answer claims a link the map doesn't
support, it's flagged for a closer look.

126
00:08:32.453 --> 00:08:35.879
A fluent answer is
not the same as a safe one.

127
00:08:35.879 --> 00:08:39.454
So the tool is built
around five safety pillars.

128
00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:45.250
First, evidence badges, so the user
can hear how strong the evidence is. Second,

129
00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.655
emergency detection in five languages,

130
00:08:47.655 --> 00:08:51.832
which tells the user to call
one zero eight, the emergency number,

131
00:08:51.832 --> 00:08:56.452
right away, and flags the call
as urgent for clinicians to review. Third,

132
00:08:56.452 --> 00:09:00.439
medication reminders by voice call,
confirmed with a key press.

133
00:09:01.153 --> 00:09:05.310
Fourth, replies kept under
thirty seconds. And fifth, a handoff:

134
00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:10.637
when a person is needed, the tool records a
request for a supervising clinician to

135
00:09:10.637 --> 00:09:11.286
follow up.

136
00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:17.219
An emergency, or a direct request
for a person, triggers that request at once.

137
00:09:17.219 --> 00:09:22.150
A complex question or emotional
distress can prompt the tool to offer one.

138
00:09:22.886 --> 00:09:25.543
And one rule sits in a box of its own.

139
00:09:25.543 --> 00:09:29.808
The tool is designed never to give
specific medication doses.

140
00:09:29.808 --> 00:09:34.143
For low-confidence answers,
it advises talking to a physician.

141
00:09:34.953 --> 00:09:39.484
Above all of this sits one principle.
She stays the decision-maker.

142
00:09:39.484 --> 00:09:42.121
The tool gives information, not orders.

143
00:09:43.153 --> 00:09:47.230
And people check the machine. Regularly,
clinicians review conversations:

144
00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:48.905
five percent picked at random,

145
00:09:48.905 --> 00:09:52.255
half of those flagged, and
every single one marked critical.

146
00:09:53.887 --> 00:09:58.831
What follows is completely fictional.
These conversations are synthetic and made up,

147
00:09:58.831 --> 00:10:00.126
with no real patients,

148
00:10:00.126 --> 00:10:04.835
families or health workers, but they
are realistic illustrations of how the tool

149
00:10:04.835 --> 00:10:05.895
works in practice.

150
00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:12.412
First, a daughter, in Tamil.

151
00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:17.558
Caregiver (daughter): Hello… my
mother… um, she has cancer.

152
00:10:17.558 --> 00:10:23.867
Chemo and all finished six months back, but
now, every night, she cries with pain…

153
00:10:24.554 --> 00:10:28.207
Palli Sahayak: Hello. Go
on, I'm listening.

154
00:10:28.207 --> 00:10:32.031
How many days has
this night pain been there?

155
00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:36.191
Caregiver (daughter): A
week… no, ten days.

156
00:10:36.191 --> 00:10:42.171
She used to make murukku for everyone…
now she won't even have one idli;

157
00:10:42.171 --> 00:10:43.666
she's got so thin.

158
00:10:44.287 --> 00:10:48.798
Palli Sahayak: She used to make
murukku… Is she drinking water now?

159
00:10:48.798 --> 00:10:50.414
Does she sleep at night?

160
00:10:50.887 --> 00:10:56.342
Caregiver (daughter): She drinks
a little water. Sleep— no, no sleep at all.

161
00:10:56.342 --> 00:10:59.644
My brother keeps calling
from Dubai every day…

162
00:11:00.154 --> 00:11:04.257
Palli Sahayak: This is a hard
time. You're looking after her well.

163
00:11:04.257 --> 00:11:07.241
Has the doctor prescribed
medicine for the pain?

164
00:11:08.054 --> 00:11:10.294
Caregiver (daughter): Yes, they have.

165
00:11:10.294 --> 00:11:15.318
But… we only give it when the pain comes, the
lady next door said she'd get used to

166
00:11:15.318 --> 00:11:15.500
it.

167
00:11:16.687 --> 00:11:21.531
Palli Sahayak: Give it just
as the doctor said. If times are set,

168
00:11:21.531 --> 00:11:25.854
don't wait for the pain.
Don't change the amount yourself;

169
00:11:25.854 --> 00:11:28.314
if you're unsure, ask the doctor.

170
00:11:28.587 --> 00:11:34.758
Caregiver (daughter): Okay… okay. Food— should
I push her to eat at least a little?

171
00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:40.665
Palli Sahayak: Don't force her.
Give small amounts of what she likes.

172
00:11:40.665 --> 00:11:44.767
If her mouth gets dry,
give little sips of water often.

173
00:11:45.454 --> 00:11:46.900
Caregiver (daughter):

174
00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:51.376
Mm… wait, one more thing… when should
I take her to the hospital?

175
00:11:51.954 --> 00:11:55.423
Palli Sahayak: Talk
to your nurse or doctor today.

176
00:11:55.423 --> 00:11:57.991
If the pain suddenly gets much worse,

177
00:11:57.991 --> 00:12:03.681
call 108 for emergency help right away, and
tell your nurse too. You're not alone.

178
00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:10.879
Next, an ASHA worker on her rounds in
Uttar Pradesh asks in Hindi about two of her

179
00:12:10.879 --> 00:12:11.679
households.

180
00:12:12.287 --> 00:12:16.993
ASHA worker: Hello… this is the
ASHA didi speaking. Um… it's like this,

181
00:12:16.993 --> 00:12:21.833
there's a cancer patient in the
village, he's really suffering with pain.

182
00:12:22.353 --> 00:12:26.479
Palli Sahayak: Hello, didi.
Since when has the pain been there?

183
00:12:26.479 --> 00:12:27.920
Can he sleep at night?

184
00:12:28.753 --> 00:12:32.225
ASHA worker: Two
weeks… no, twenty days now.

185
00:12:32.225 --> 00:12:35.775
He groans all night, and
he's stopped eating.

186
00:12:35.775 --> 00:12:39.800
The road was flooded
today, I barely made it there.

187
00:12:40.987 --> 00:12:44.770
Palli Sahayak: You made it through the
water, that was good of you.

188
00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:48.554
Has a doctor prescribed pain medicine?
How is the family giving it?

189
00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:54.635
ASHA worker: It's prescribed… hmm, but the
family gives it only now and then,

190
00:12:54.635 --> 00:12:58.157
they say he'll get hooked. And
they force him to eat.

191
00:12:58.920 --> 00:12:59.854
Palli Sahayak:

192
00:12:59.854 --> 00:13:05.056
Give the medicine exactly as the doctor
wrote it, and don't change the amount.

193
00:13:05.056 --> 00:13:09.859
If they worry about getting hooked, talk
to the doctor. No forcing food,

194
00:13:09.859 --> 00:13:11.927
just a little of what he likes.

195
00:13:12.187 --> 00:13:13.653
ASHA worker: Yes, yes… got it.

196
00:13:13.653 --> 00:13:17.711
Okay, in the other house there's a grandfather,
an old breathing illness, two steps

197
00:13:17.711 --> 00:13:18.836
and he's out of breath.

198
00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:24.957
Palli Sahayak: Can he talk comfortably
right now? His lips aren't blue?

199
00:13:25.587 --> 00:13:33.371
ASHA worker: He's talking… his
lips— wait, let me look… no, they're fine.

200
00:13:33.371 --> 00:13:34.971
He just panics.

201
00:13:35.453 --> 00:13:39.442
Palli Sahayak: Sit him
upright, leaning slightly forward.

202
00:13:39.442 --> 00:13:41.401
Keep a fan towards his face.

203
00:13:41.401 --> 00:13:46.860
Breathe out slowly through pursed
lips, and take medicines just as prescribed.

204
00:13:47.387 --> 00:13:52.067
ASHA worker: Okay… Listen, when
should I call the doctor?

205
00:13:52.067 --> 00:13:54.613
I mean— how long should I wait?

206
00:13:55.420 --> 00:14:00.145
Palli Sahayak: Both families should talk
to their nurse or doctor today.

207
00:14:00.145 --> 00:14:01.326
If lips turn blue,

208
00:14:01.326 --> 00:14:06.052
there's confusion, he can't speak, or the
pain suddenly gets much worse,

209
00:14:06.052 --> 00:14:10.187
call 108 for emergency help
right away, and tell the nurse too.

210
00:14:11.952 --> 00:14:16.653
And a patient himself, in Punjabi, a
language not yet switched on.

211
00:14:17.285 --> 00:14:18.109
Patient:

212
00:14:18.109 --> 00:14:26.659
Sat Sri Akal ji… um… I've got this old
breathing illness, I walk a little and I get

213
00:14:26.659 --> 00:14:27.792
breathless.

214
00:14:28.552 --> 00:14:32.839
Palli Sahayak: Sat Sri Akal
ji. Can you talk comfortably right now?

215
00:14:33.519 --> 00:14:36.274
Patient: Sitting down it's fine.

216
00:14:36.274 --> 00:14:43.161
But at night… a sort of panic comes,
my grandson has exams, I don't want to wake

217
00:14:43.161 --> 00:14:43.505
him.

218
00:14:44.085 --> 00:14:48.507
Palli Sahayak: You think of your
grandson, that's kind.

219
00:14:48.507 --> 00:14:53.412
For how many days has it
been worse? Any fever or chest pain?

220
00:14:54.052 --> 00:14:58.252
Patient: Three… no,
four days. No fever, no pain.

221
00:14:58.252 --> 00:15:03.738
I just went to feed the buffalo, and
got breathless right there.

222
00:15:04.219 --> 00:15:07.566
Palli Sahayak: Are you taking
the medicines the doctor gave you?

223
00:15:08.085 --> 00:15:16.231
Patient: Yes ji, I take them… but
sometimes— to tell the truth, I forget.

224
00:15:17.052 --> 00:15:21.709
Palli Sahayak: Take the
medicines just as the doctor said.

225
00:15:21.709 --> 00:15:24.037
I can call you to remind you.

226
00:15:24.285 --> 00:15:30.332
Patient: Yes, yes… that's good. And at night,
when the panic comes, what do I do?

227
00:15:31.319 --> 00:15:34.956
Palli Sahayak: Sit upright,
leaning slightly forward.

228
00:15:34.956 --> 00:15:39.966
Keep a fan towards your face, and breathe
out slowly through pursed lips.

229
00:15:40.752 --> 00:15:45.845
Patient: Alright ji… one minute,
there was something else… yes,

230
00:15:45.845 --> 00:15:48.189
what else should I watch for?

231
00:15:48.852 --> 00:15:52.246
Palli Sahayak: Talk
to your nurse or doctor today.

232
00:15:52.246 --> 00:15:56.115
If your lips turn blue,
you feel confused or can't speak,

233
00:15:56.115 --> 00:16:00.459
call 108 for emergency help
right away, and tell your nurse too.

234
00:16:01.519 --> 00:16:06.242
Again, these were fictional, but they show
the pattern: short questions first,

235
00:16:06.242 --> 00:16:10.662
then comfort, medicines only as
prescribed, the danger signs, and always,

236
00:16:10.662 --> 00:16:12.781
talk to your nurse or doctor today.

237
00:16:13.519 --> 00:16:17.989
Punjabi is already among the languages
the speech service handles.

238
00:16:17.989 --> 00:16:23.543
So adding a language like this is mostly
configuration, not a new app or new code,

239
00:16:23.543 --> 00:16:25.439
as long as two things exist:

240
00:16:25.439 --> 00:16:30.789
local care guidelines for the library, and
support for the language in the live

241
00:16:30.789 --> 00:16:33.363
voice model or a speech-to-text model.

242
00:16:33.363 --> 00:16:38.781
Each new language would still be tested and
approved by clinicians before anyone

243
00:16:38.781 --> 00:16:39.323
uses it.

244
00:16:40.951 --> 00:16:43.230
Those conversations were made up.

245
00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:47.234
So here is the one real
check the answers have had so far.

246
00:16:47.951 --> 00:16:48.808
In phase one,

247
00:16:48.808 --> 00:16:54.145
four palliative care doctors, two from Max
Healthcare and two from Pallium India,

248
00:16:54.145 --> 00:16:56.978
each reviewed the
same forty made-up cases:

249
00:16:56.978 --> 00:17:00.338
twenty of advanced lung
cancer, and twenty of COPD.

250
00:17:01.051 --> 00:17:03.454
That's one hundred and sixty reviews.

251
00:17:03.454 --> 00:17:08.650
Each doctor read one written English
answer per case, not the voice, and not the

252
00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:09.689
other languages.

253
00:17:10.918 --> 00:17:15.562
On average, they scored the
answers seven point eight out of ten.

254
00:17:15.562 --> 00:17:21.278
In ninety-five of the hundred and sixty
reviews, a doctor said: use it as it is.

255
00:17:22.518 --> 00:17:23.533
But look closer.

256
00:17:23.533 --> 00:17:28.480
All four doctors said yes together
for only three of the forty cases, and they

257
00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:30.573
often disagreed with one another.

258
00:17:30.573 --> 00:17:35.647
In twelve cases, at least one doctor
gave safety a low score, and their comments

259
00:17:35.647 --> 00:17:39.769
raised concerns about emergency
escalation and medication advice.

260
00:17:41.084 --> 00:17:46.428
Good scores on average, with real safety
worries in the details. The work continues:

261
00:17:46.428 --> 00:17:51.453
flagged answers will be revised, and Palli
Sahayak has not yet been tested with

262
00:17:51.453 --> 00:17:52.344
real patients.

263
00:17:54.184 --> 00:17:59.271
Go back to that window, and that
question. Her pain is worse at night.

264
00:17:59.271 --> 00:18:00.579
What should we do?

265
00:18:01.417 --> 00:18:04.042
Next time that question is asked at night,

266
00:18:04.042 --> 00:18:09.291
there may be a voice that answers in
her language, with a doctor one step behind it.

267
00:18:09.291 --> 00:18:13.852
Whether it helps is something we're now
setting out to measure carefully.

268
00:18:15.284 --> 00:18:20.211
Palli Sahayak is open source.
Anyone can read the code, and use it.
